## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical integration of adapters in a multi-tenant infra context, focusing on isolation, policy routing, and real-world constraints (GPU, data, latency).

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant adapters and routing
- Injection points: attention first, optional FFN
- Quantization and PEFT variants trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation sketch for adapter injection
function applyAdapters(model, tenantId){
  const adapter = loadAdapter(tenantId);
  model.insertAdapterAt('attention', adapter);
  return model;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle adapter drift across tenants?
- What metrics would you monitor for policy compliance over time?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a real-time moderation workflow with multi-tool planning, guardrails, and production observability on large platforms like Coinbase or Discord.

## Key Concepts

- LangChain agent orchestration with AutoGen planning
- Real-time event streams, idempotency, and dedup
- Tool integration and conditional workflows (SentimentAPI, RiskScoreAPI, ModerationTool)
- Observability, privacy, rate limits, and fault tolerance

## Code Example

```javascript
// high-level skeleton illustrating the planner invocation and routing
async function handleDiscordEvent(evt){
  const plan = await autoGenPlan({event: evt});
  for(const step of plan.steps){
    await executeTool(step.tool, step.args);
  }
  logOutcome(evt.id);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the latency budgets under burst traffic?
- How would you recover from partial tool failures without data loss?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a scalable, multilingual vector-search pipeline with practical constraints like latency, freshness, and safety.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented generation with embeddings
- Semantic chunking with overlap and size bounds
- Vector DB selection (HNSW, indexing, sharding)
- Cross-encoder reranking for result quality
- Update pipelines and data freshness guards

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: indexing
function indexDocs(docs){
  for (const d of docs){
    const chunks = chunk(d.text, 800, 100); // size, overlap
    for (const c of chunks){
      const embedding = embed(c);
      await vectorDB.store(embedding, c);
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a candidate’s ability to design end-to-end evaluation for private, privacy-conscious enterprise assistants, focusing on faithfulness, hallucination, and safety under real-world data/versioning constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Grounding to versioned sources and source IDs
- Hallucination detection in live responses
- Privacy safety: PII/secret leakage checks in output and citations
- Performance metrics under load and latency budgets

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal evaluation script outline (pseudo)
function evaluate(model, corpus) {
  const prompts = loadPrompts();
  return prompts.map(p => {
    const out = model.respond(p);
    const grounded = ground(out, corpus, p);
    return { prompt: p, output: out, grounded };
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect partial grounding failures (e.g., only some claims anchored)?
- How would you integrate this into a CI/CD pipeline with private data access controls?
